  "conditions": {
    "kernel": "Complete native ae_codimension call in a fresh process, after parsing, before JSON output, timed by std::chrono::steady_clock. No CLI startup or parsing in kernel_ns. All completed calls verified the published finite dimension and stopped with a certified tail.",
    "limits": "2 GiB virtual address space and 120 seconds elapsed per process. Timed-out trials have no kernel result. Process memory and CPU fields include startup and parsing.",
    "order": "Baseline calls for different maps overlapped on distinct physical cores, two attempts per presentation and five more for the completed Ahat4. After timings use 9 samples per input in reversing order; inputs run sequentially on the same logical CPU per map as the baseline.",
    "reproduce": "Build make -C native -f groebner.mk build/bench_ae_codimension. Pipe variables joined by commas, then one map component on each following line to native/build/bench_ae_codimension 1 solve-once. Pin the process to the listed logical CPU with taskset -c and set ulimit -v 2097152 with timeout 120.",
    "thread_cpu_control": "A separate comparison times exactly the solve-once kernel call with CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID on logical CPU 1. Five trials per variant and presentation; builds and inputs alternate in reverse order. The previous solver uses identical native arithmetic to the baseline with relaxed work guards to allow it to complete. The 40000 projected matrix coefficient-bits times rank limit becomes 400000, the 4096-bit dual limit becomes 16384, the cell and projection-update totals as well as the compressed pullback term budget increase tenfold. The updated solver uses its production work limits.",
    "smaller_map_controls": "Warmed CPU-time comparison of the stock and updated libraries with production limits, on logical CPU 13. Five fresh-process batches per map and build, reversing build order and input order on alternate rounds. Two warm-up calls per process followed by 15 measured solves on Ahat2, Bhat3, Ahat3 or 5 measured solves on each Bhat5 presentation. The samples are nanoseconds per solve. Inputs are published with the earlier Sharland benchmarks."
